 "text": "ONLINE COPYRIGHT INFRINGEMENT\nIN THE EUROPEAN UNION\nFILMS, MUSIC, PUBLICATIONS, SOFTWARE AND TV\n(2017-2023)\nNovember 2024\nTRENDS IN DIGITAL COPYRIGHT INFRINGEMENT IN THE EUROPEAN UNION\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2\nONLINE COPYRIGHT INFRINGEMENT IN THE EUROPEAN UNION\nFILMS, MUSIC, PUBLICATIONS, SOFTWARE AND TV (2017-2023)\nTB-01-24-000-EN-N ISBN: 978-92-9156-363-0 DOI: 10.2814/3685063\n© European Union Intellectual Property Office, 2024\nReuse is allowed provided the source is acknowledged and changes are mentioned (CC BY 4.0)\nTRENDS IN DIGITAL COPYRIGHT INFRINGEMENT IN THE EUROPEAN UNION\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3\nPROJECT TEAM\nDamian BORNAS CAYUELA, Economics and Data Intelligence Research Specialist\nAmani Romaissa DJAIL, Economics and Data Intelligence Trainee\nNathan WAJSMAN, Chief Economist\nTRENDS IN DIGITAL COPYRIGHT INFRINGEMENT IN THE EUROPEAN UNION\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n4\nForeword\nOnline copyright infringement remains a pressing concern for rightsholders and society at large. The \ncreative industry's very existence relies on the ability of artists and creators to receive fair \ncompensation for their work. Without it, the diversity of content available to consumers may dwindle, \nultimately affecting the fabric of our cultural landscape.\nBuilding on the foundation laid by our previous studies, published in 2019, 2021 and 2023, this report \npresents an updated analysis of online copyright infringement in the European Union. The research \nundertaken by the EUIPO has consistently shown that the dynamics of piracy are complex and \nmultifaceted, with trends shifting over time.\nThis latest study extends the dataset to the end of 2023, providing an even more comprehensive \nunderstanding of the current state of online copyright infringement. It also introduces new features, \nincluding an in-depth examination of the piracy of sports and other live events, as well as the \ninclusion of data on software piracy for 2021-2023. These additions enhance the EUIPO’s ability to \ninform evidence-based policy decisions and guide the development of effective strategies to combat \nonline piracy and to support the European Commission in the implementation of its Recommendation \non combatting online piracy of sports and other live events, published in May 2023.\nAs we move forward, it is essential that we continue to refine our understanding of the intricate \nrelationships between piracy, consumer behaviour and the creative industry. By doing so, we \ncontribute to the objective of ensuring that artists and creators receive the compensation they \ndeserve, and that consumers have access to a diverse range of high-quality content.\nTRENDS IN DIGITAL COPYRIGHT INFRINGEMENT IN THE EUROPEAN UNION\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n5\nAcknowledgements\nThe authors are grateful to Antanina Garanasvili, Lecturer in Economics, London Metropolitan \nUniversity, and Brent Lutes, Chief Economist of the US Copyright Office for their comments on an \nearlier draft of this report.\nTRENDS IN DIGITAL COPYRIGHT INFRINGEMENT IN THE EUROPEAN UNION\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n6\nTable of Contents\nForeword ...............................................................................